        fp: "A plaintiff was operating an automobile across an elevated municipal bridge during an unseasonably heavy evening thunderstorm. A private municipal utility contractor had parked a heavy flatbed maintenance truck in the right lane of the bridge to clear a storm drain. In direct violation of state highway safety regulations, the utility crew failed to set out reflective orange cones, failed to activate the truck's overhead flashing yellow warning light bar, and positioned no advance warning signs on the bridge approach. The plaintiff, driving at 35 mph (the lawful speed limit), rounded a blind curve on the bridge. Due to heavy sheet rain, darkness, and the absence of any warning lights, the plaintiff was unable to perceive the stationary truck until he was thirty feet away. The plaintiff slammed on his brakes, but his vehicle hydroplaned on standing water and struck the rear of the maintenance truck. The impact fractured the plaintiff's collarbone and ribs. In the plaintiff's negligence action against the utility contractor, the contractor introduced testimony that five seconds before the collision, the plaintiff had glanced down at his dashboard navigation screen, and moved for a directed verdict based on the plaintiff's contributory fault.",
